; The project verdict aliases, once, at the top of the test tree. None of
; them runs by default: every lib/*/dune carries two inert instrumentation
; stanzas, and both backends stay switched off until --instrument-with asks
; for them, so an ordinary `dune runtest` pays nothing for any of this.
;
;   dune build @cover  --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.coverage
;   WINDTRAP_MUTATE=1 dune runtest --force --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.mutate
;   dune build @mutate
;   dune build @admit --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.mutate
;
; The first two are asymmetric on purpose. Coverage accumulates as a side
; effect of any instrumented run, so @cover both runs the suites and merges.
; A mutation *verdict* only exists if a run was asked to test mutants
; (WINDTRAP_MUTATE=1), so @mutate merges what previous runs left rather than
; producing them -- and the merge is what makes the answer right: a library
; here is covered by several test executables, per-executable reports
; disagree by construction, and `windtrap mutate` merges them under
; killed-anywhere-wins. Trust the merged report, never a per-suite one.
;
; (universe) is load-bearing in both: the .coverage and .mutants files test
; executables write at exit are not declarable dependencies, so it makes the
; milliseconds-cheap merge re-run on every build. `chdir %{workspace_root}`
; keeps the rules correct from wherever they are invoked.

(rule
 (alias cover)
 (deps
  (alias_rec runtest)
  (universe))
 (action
  (chdir
   %{workspace_root}
   (run %{bin:windtrap} coverage --min 60))))

(rule
 (alias mutate)
 (deps (universe))
 (action
  (chdir
   %{workspace_root}
   (run %{bin:windtrap} mutate))))

; The admission alias is the only one that runs rather than merges:
; WINDTRAP_MUTATE=admit with no selection judges every test the run
; executes, so it asks "can every test in this suite fail?" with no filter
; to keep in sync. An admission run persists nothing, so its rulings are the
; whole product and it never perturbs @mutate.
;
; Named suites only. One omission is structural, from SKILL 3: a suite that
; tests its subject through a process it spawns has nothing to admit, because
; the arming never reaches the child. That rules out the whole blackbox tree,
; the pty tree, and the unit suites whose subject is a real child process
; (test_llm_local, test_ocaml_dune_rpc).
;
; The other omission is a backlog, and it is deliberate that this alias starts
; green rather than starting complete: an alias that is red on the day it
; lands teaches that a red alias is normal, which is the opposite of what
; UNJUSTIFIED means. test_config admits 56 of 66 with 10 NO SITES and nothing
; unjustified. The other three in-process suites do not yet, and each ruling
; is a real finding to clear before it joins this list:
;
;   test_permission   78 admitted, 7 unjustified of 103
;   test_protocol     71 admitted, 2 unjustified of  78
;   test_session     200 admitted, 2 unjustified of 206
;
; The seven in test_permission are one `cases` table (test_permission.ml:1739)
; whose every row reaches `version <> 1` in lib/permission/answer.ml:98 and no
; row exercises it: the missing test is an answer whose version is not 1, not
; a weak assertion in the rows that exist. Audit them with
;
;   WINDTRAP_MUTATE=admit dune exec --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.mutate \
;     test/unit/test_permission.exe

(rule
 (alias admit)
 (deps (universe) unit/test_config.exe)
 (action
  (setenv
   WINDTRAP_MUTATE
   admit
   (run %{exe:unit/test_config.exe}))))
